WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.984
When a user has lost
their only strong method,

2
00:00:02.984 --> 00:00:08.138
re-registration alone leaves a gap: whoever
signs in next sets up the new method.

3
00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:11.012
A Temporary Access Pass closes that gap.

4
00:00:11.012 --> 00:00:14.826
The sequence on the slide is the
heart of it: re-proof the person,

5
00:00:14.826 --> 00:00:19.538
revoke their sessions, remove the lost
method, then issue a one-time pass with

6
00:00:19.538 --> 00:00:24.250
the shortest lifetime the handover
allows, straight to the verified person.

7
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:28.506
Stay with them while they register
something phishing-resistant, then tell

8
00:00:28.506 --> 00:00:32.762
them on another channel, so a victim
learns about a reset they never asked for.

9
00:00:32.762 --> 00:00:35.888
The table shows the pass's
own limits worth knowing.

10
00:00:35.888 --> 00:00:39.220
Some tasks, though, need
more than one approver.
